  <!-- SEO -->
  <div class="seo-content">
    <h2>Roman Numerals — Complete Guide</h2>
    <p>Roman numerals use seven letters from the Latin alphabet to represent numbers: I (1), V (5), X (10), L (50), C (100), D (500), and M (1000). The system was used throughout the Roman Empire and remains in use today for clock faces, book chapters, movie sequels, year numbering, and formal documents.</p>

    <h3>The Subtractive Principle</h3>
    <p>When a smaller numeral appears before a larger one, it is subtracted. Only six subtractive combinations are valid: IV=4, IX=9, XL=40, XC=90, CD=400, CM=900. All other numbers are additive — VII = 5+1+1 = 7, XII = 10+1+1 = 12.</p>

    <h3>Roman Numerals in Daily Life</h3>
    <p>Roman numerals appear on clock faces (XII for 12), Super Bowl numbers (Super Bowl LVIII), Olympic Games, film sequels (Rocky IV), copyright years in movies, chapter numbers in books, and formal outlines. Understanding them is a practical life skill!</p>

    <h3>Quick Conversion Tips</h3>
    <p>To convert to Roman numerals, break the number into thousands, hundreds, tens, and units. For 1994: 1000=M, 900=CM, 90=XC, 4=IV → MCMXCIV. For 2024: 2000=MM, 20=XX, 4=IV → MMXXIV.</p>

